Logic :

solved this problem using **backtracking** because I need to generate all possible combinations.
I start from number **1** and try every possible choice. Whenever I choose a number, I add it to my current list.
Then I recursively choose the next numbers starting from **`i + 1`**, so I don't pick the same number again or generate duplicate combinations like `[2,1]` after `[1,2]`.
Whenever my list size becomes **`k`**, it means I have found one valid combination, so I store a copy of it.
After returning from recursion, I remove the last element from the list. This is the **backtracking (undo)** step, which helps me try the next possible number.
I also used a small optimization called **pruning**. Instead of looping till `n`, I stop early because if there aren't enough numbers left to complete a combination of size `k`, exploring that branch is unnecessary.
This reduces extra recursive calls and makes the solution more efficient.


Code :

class Solution {
    List<List<Integer>> ans=new ArrayList<>();
    public List<List<Integer>> combine(int n, int k) {
        comb(1,n,k,new ArrayList<>());
        return ans;
    }
    void  comb(int index,int n,int k,List<Integer> list){
        if(list.size()==k){
            ans.add(new ArrayList<>(list));
            return;
        }
        for(int i=index;i<= n-k+list.size()+1;i++){
            list.add(i);
            comb(i+1,n,k,list);
            list.remove(list.size()-1);
        }
    }
}
